{"labs": [{"stimuli": [{"id": 0, "type": "welcome", "_data": [], "template_override": 1, "contents": [{}]}, {"title": "", "id": 1, "_data": [], "type": "audio", "template_override": 1, "contents": [{"type": "slideshow", "value": {"images": [{"image": "engenn6le02ob06re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "time": "0", "text": ""}, {"image": "engenn6le02ob06re2img01.jpg", "id": 1, "time": "100", "text": "Throughout the years following independence, Gandhi became a national political figure. She was president of her party and served as the Minister of Information and Broadcasting in the government. In 1966, Gandhi was elected as the first woman prime minister of India."}, {"image": "engenn6le02ob06re2img02.jpg", "id": 2, "time": "17900", "text": "For many Indians, Gandhi used to be a symbol of hope in India. Her face and her trademark, the famous streak of gray hair, would become the symbol of stability in the country. They saw her as the \"Mataji,\" or Great Mother of India. To her critics, however, Gandhi was a dictator who would only think of her own popularity before the best interests of the country."}, {"image": "engenn6le02ob06re2img03.jpg", "id": 3, "time": "43000", "text": "Although Gandhi was popular among the majority of Indians, she was disliked by some factions within her country. One of these groups was the Sikhs, a religious group with 13 million followers. In 1984, Gandhi was assassinated by her own Sikh bodyguards. Her son, Rajiv Gandhi, succeeded her as prime minister until 1989."}, {"image": "engenn6le02ob06re2img03.jpg", "id": 4, "time": "67000", "text": "Although Gandhi has remained a controversial figure in Indian politics, she is remembered as someone who has greatly influenced the developing character of India."}], "coverimg": "engenn6le02ob06re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "engenn6le02ob06re2aud01"}}]}], "id": 0, "_data": [{"type": "lab_options", "contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}]}], "type": "scho